This branch implements 3 new test cases:
* Sudoku solver- benchmarks the jvm by solving 33761 unique sudoku puzzles, read from a txt file
* Autodiff- compatibility with a real Java library, and a simple benchmark
* Simulated input/output- the ability for bjvm to interface with fake provided stdin data for a test case

This branch also implements features necessary for basic I/O to work:
FileDescriptor, FileInputStream, and FileOutputStream natives have been updated so that io works using direct syscalls, and stdio can be simulated when set to `capture_stdio` is set to true. When used, test cases can now be provided with a string for standard input data instead of reading from the real file descriptor, similar to that of Codeforces problems.

The following function types are defined by bjvm:
```c
typedef void (*bjvm_write_bytes)(char * buf, int len, void *param);
typedef int (*bjvm_read_bytes)(char * buf, int len, void *param);
typedef int (*bjvm_poll_available_bytes)(void *param);
```
These functions are used as jvm options:
```c
// Overrides for stdio (if nullptr, uses the default implementation)
bjvm_read_bytes  read_stdin;
bjvm_poll_available_bytes poll_available_stdin;
bjvm_write_bytes write_stdout;
bjvm_write_bytes write_stderr;

void *stdio_override_param;
```
When these are set to the null pointer value, then the jni bindings will rely on the proper unix file descriptor syscalls to do their task.

The Async natives test still passes on this branch.

